The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (message_sequence_number identifies a transaction which is still pending) {
    fetch related payload;
}
```
```c
channel ownership remains with the platform until eventually a late reply is received;
```
```c
spurious IRQ related to the late reply can be wrongly associated with the freshly enqueued request;
```
Reason:
The bug occurs due to a race condition where a late reply from the platform is incorrectly linked to a new transaction. The channel ownership remains with the platform until the late reply is processed, which can lead to the message header in the shared memory area being updated with the new request instead of the delayed response. This misassociation causes the SCMI stack to misinterpret the spurious interrupt as a valid reply, leading to incorrect message processing.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (cl->knows_txdone && !shmem_channel_free(smbox->shmem)) {
```
```c
dev_warn(smbox->cinfo->dev, "Ignoring spurious A2P IRQ !\n");
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a consistency check that verifies whether the channel is free before processing the interrupt. If the channel is still owned by the platform (i.e., not free), the spurious interrupt is ignored, preventing the SCMI stack from misinterpreting it as a valid reply. This effectively addresses the race condition by ensuring that only valid interrupts are processed when the channel is in the correct state.

